I"F<h1 id="a-deep-learning-approach-for-the-reconstruction-of-the-great-lakes-ice-cover-spatial-distribution-using-historical-land-based-temperature-observation">A Deep Learning Approach for the Reconstruction of the Great Lakes Ice Cover Spatial Distribution Using Historical Land-Based Temperature Observation</h1>

<p>Immediately after graduating in May 2025, I was lucky enough to land a fellowship at the <a href="https://ciglr.seas.umich.edu/">Cooperative Institute for Great Lakes Research</a> (CIGLR) in Ann Arbor, MI. CIGLR is a NOAA cooperative institute, meaning it is officially part of the University of Michigan School for Environment and Sustainability, but funded by NOAA OAR, and its employees (including myself at the time) are based out of the <a href="//glerl.noaa.gov">NOAA Great Lakes Environmental Research Laboratory</a> (GLERL) and work closely with NOAA researchers.</p>

<p>My lead mentor, Dr. Hazem Abdelhady, was a postdoc at CIGLR specializing in machine learning and physical modeling projects in water resource engineering. Hazem had previously built a <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhydrol.2024.132445">deep learning model</a> to predict ice concentrations in Lake Michigan based on ERA-5 reanalysis variables. ERA-5 goes back to 1940, but a longer time series of observed air temperatures at a handful of locations surrounding the Great Lakes was compiled at CIGLR going all the way back to 1897! A previous CIGLR fellow had attempted to model ice cover based on these sparse air temperature records using simple linear regression models with success at lakewide and annual resolutions, but my mentors wondered if it was possible to improve upon this initial effort using deep learning methods, and passed this task on to me.</p>

<p>While I had a decent understanding of ice geophysics and simple physical models going into this fellowship, I had no concept of deep learning models. Luckily, my mentors gave me a few weeks before starting the project to familiarize myself with these methods (<a href="https://www.deeplearningbook.org/contents/intro.html">what is deep learning?</a>) through a few online courses and readings. Due to my inexperience, I was directed to work my way up from simple, deep neural networks to convolutional and recurrent neural networks, which helped inform the final architecture of my model. I’m really grateful for the way this experience opened my eyes to deep learning and its fascinating and powerful <a href="https://doi.org/10.1002/9781119646181">applications in geoscience</a>. It is pretty easy to get jaded (and angry) about “AI” these days, so it was nice to see that this family of tools has more targeted purposes in projects like this one.</p>

<h3 id="approach">Approach</h3>

<p>The crux of this task was the conversion of sparse (1D) time series inputs into gridded (2D) time series outputs. While there are more sophisticated, existing DL architectures that are capable of this task (e.g., transformers), I stuck to the basic building blocks of DNNs, CNNs, and RNNs (LSTM, CONV-LSTM). Multilayer perceptrons (DNNs) are highly flexible and theoretically approximate <em>any</em> continuous function, but often at a high computational cost. I used a DNN to complete this transformation through the entire development process and added other network types upstream and downstream to improve temporal and spatial processing without adding computational bulk through additional DNN/fully-connected layers. Even while minimizing the use of DNNs, these layers limited my grid resolution to 18km (as opposed to the original label resolution of 1.8km).</p>

<p>Feature testing indicated that including multiple trailing averages of daily temperatures at each station in the feature set for each station greatly improved performance, possibly representing the thermal inertia of lake temperatures and its importance in lake ice formation. Additionally, adding gridded, time-independent, physical features like depth, shoreline proximity, and position also improved performance. A collection of ice charts beginning in 1972 were used for training (1972-2010) and validation (2010-2023).</p>

<p>The final architecture utilizes LSTM layers with a one week lookback period to process these time series inputs into a sort of latent space (not really), from which a DNN scales up the units and casts them onto “lake” cells in an 18km grid. The “land” cells are filled with zeros. From there, the lake cell grid for each day is joined with the gridded features (depth, etc.) and fed through CNN layers to the output. My loss functions and metrics were both masked to only consider lake cells in the output and label grids.</p>

