<h3 id="agu-abstract-and-presentation">AGU Abstract and Presentation</h3>

<p>Presented in Lakes and Inland Water Bodies at AGU 2025.</p>

<blockquote>
  <p><em>In the Laurentian Great Lakes, the timing and extent of lake ice cover plays a critical role in controlling physical and biological processes. While understanding historical ice trends is critical for anticipating continued changes in ice cover, the observational record is relatively sparse, with measurements extending only to the early 1970s. Attempts at reconstructing longer ice cover records (e.g. 100+ year) have primarily focused on lakewide averages or maxima, utilizing both physical and data-driven approaches. In this study, an original deep learning model is presented for predicting gridded ice cover concentration in the Great Lakes from coastal, land-based temperature time series. The proposed architecture leverages Long-Short-Term-Memory and Convolutional Neural Networks to capture spatial and temporal dependencies. Continuous long-term (1897 - 2023) daily air temperature records, sourced from over 20 regional weather stations, are used as the primary input for generating time series features, but gridded, time-invariant features (i.e., depth and shoreline proximity) are also incorporated into the model at an intermediate step. Ice charts beginning in 1972 were used for training, validation, and testing. Models were trained and assessed for each individual lake through lakewide average metrics and local error metrics, and lakewide-averaged predictions were compared to physical model output. Operating on an 18km grid at daily resolution, the model had a lake-wide RMSE of 4.0% and an average local RMSE of 12.8% across all lakes and validation dates. Importantly, this model performs as well or better than other operational forecast models used in the region, highlighting the dominating control of air temperature on ice formation in the Great Lakes. The developed model is used to produce hindcasts of spatially explicit daily ice cover on all five lakes between 1897 and 2024, facilitating novel analysis of multidecadal trends in ice cover over the 20th century.</em></p>
</blockquote>

